| Date | Task | Description of AI Tool Usage | Output | Integration | Rationale | Challenges and Considerations |
|------|------|-------------------------------|--------|-------------|-----------|-------------------------------|
| 2026-06-25 | Frontend interface | Used Codex to help design and implement the frontend chat interface, search forms, result cards, and styling for the student travel chatbot. | Suggestions and code structure for `frontend/index.html`, `frontend/src/app.js`, and `frontend/src/styles.css`, including chat bubbles, API calls, tabbed search, loading/error states, and responsive layout. | Reviewed and adapted the generated ideas into the project frontend, then connected the UI to the Flask endpoints for chat, flights, rides, and hostels. | AI support helped speed up UI implementation and produce a cleaner first version of the interface while keeping the frontend lightweight. | Needed human review to ensure API paths matched the backend, request bodies included the required fields, generated code stayed understandable, and the UI remained appropriate for a student travel tool. |
| 2026-06-23 | Groq system prompt | Used Claude to help draft and refine the Groq system prompt for Roam, the student budget travel assistant. | A concise system prompt defining Roam's role, tone, budget-first behavior, honesty about mock or missing data, and response length constraints. | Integrated the refined prompt into `backend/services/llm.py` as `SYSTEM_PROMPT` for every Groq chat completion request. | The prompt makes the chatbot's behavior more consistent and aligned with the project goal: practical, low-budget travel advice for students. | Required manual adjustment to avoid overpromising real-time prices, keep recommendations grounded in available data, and make the assistant transparent when flight, ride, or hostel data is mocked. |
